                             THE KURT ETHOS

Kurt is a low-level, general-purpose programming language for systems
programming. It exists to reach where other languages stop: the level
closest to the machine, where the programmer must be able to say exactly
what happens.

This document states what Kurt is and what it must remain.


                                Identity

Kurt is, and shall remain, a low-level systems language: it does not
stand between the programmer and the machine, and it is bound to no
vendor, platform, or era. A language raised above the machine — managed,
hosted, or otherwise removed from it — is not Kurt, whatever it is
called.


                               Principles

    1. The machine is a machine. A machine is caused to act by every
       condition, and alike by the same, and Kurt describes it as it
       is. That something went wrong never makes what happened
       meaningless: the machine still acted, and Kurt does not deny it.

    2. The specification is sovereign. The specification is the sole
       definition of Kurt, and nothing else is. A language that adopts
       the constraints of its tools as its own becomes their servant.
       Where the specification defines behaviour, that behaviour holds.
       Where it cannot fix a result, it shall say so rather than remain
       silent.

    3. Transparency. Special semantics arises only from a special
       mechanism; an ordinary mechanism carries only ordinary meaning.
       A construct means what the specification or the programmer
       defines and nothing else, with nothing settled out of sight.

    4. The fewest assumptions. Kurt assumes as little as it can and
       states what it assumes. It does not ask the programmer to trust
       what it has not written down.

    5. Acceptance. Every action carries intent; to call something an
       error requires context, and prohibition requires reason. So Kurt
       does not forbid by arbitrary judgement: it permits the dangerous,
       does not condemn it, and makes the danger known.

    6. Simplicity. Where the principles above leave a choice, take the
       simplest and most beautiful design. Kurt is not bound by legacy,
       and carries none of it forward.


                               Permanence

The identity and principles above are perpetual in substance. Their
wording may be refined for more precise expression, but the philosophy
they embody may never be weakened, narrowed, or set aside, whatever the
reviser’s authority; nor may refinement serve as a disguise for doing
so. A revision that violates this merely produces something that is not
Kurt.


                                Standing

This document states the philosophy; the philosophy binds. Every
property of Kurt is bound by it, and whatever contradicts it is no
longer Kurt. A corporation or other power may fork the specification
under a name of its own, but it cannot make what is not Kurt and call
it Kurt. Kurt is defined by the philosophy, not by who holds it.
